<p><strong>I am not a verified reviewer</strong></p>
<p>G'day how are you going. Let's take a look at what you've got here</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Is&nbsp;it&nbsp;a&nbsp;bird,&nbsp;is&nbsp;it&nbsp;a&nbsp;sleigh...</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;no&nbsp;it's&nbsp;Yule&nbsp;Man!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I may have used &quot;quickly&quot; a bit too casually there, when I mostly meant to try and get the family as a whole introduced to the readers as early on in the story as I can manage. Certainly wouldn't do to have someone show up halfway in, after all!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ok so with introducing the family, well that should be one of the first things you do. I know you said as early as you can manage, but the only time you would ever not introduce them first, is if you are introducing the villain first, so in this case Yule Man. If you don't plan on starting with the Yule Man first in your story then definitely start with introducing the family, that way it gives the readers time to familiarise themselves with the family, and if you do it right, like or dislike the family.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Possibly tying in with the above point about some sort of family frictions that could be addressed during the event itself.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This could be good. What you could do is have the younger kid be known for lying and being mischievous, so his parents don't believe him straight away, only when the older kid tells them that they start worrying. After that I reckon what EthanHanson said about it maybe being a neighbour could be good.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I like the idea of using the house and property to try and hide from him, maybe let the family come to some wrong conclusions about how to stop him…</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a great idea to use. What you could do is have it start outside, like in a barn, shed, forest, where they just get away, clambering into the house and locking the doors. From there you can do what you said and have it like a deadly game of hide and seek (cause who doesn't love hide and seek).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Regarding the 'happiest ending', you make another pretty good point. Although, depending on how early in his collecting he hits this family, just the fact that multiple small children are recovered from the sack he brought with him could automatically make it a pretty happy ending regardless… excepting potential pet deaths and the background knowledge that the Yule Man brutally murdered the families of each of those children and made them watch…</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is all up to you. I would recommend killing someone or something in it, other than the previous victims of course. Like maybe a pet saves one of the kids but does doing so it dies. You'd mainly do this to add to help show the danger the family is in, since if you haven't read the article you wounld't know what the Yule Man is and how dangerous they can be. By killing someone or something it helps show that, it also shows what the stakes are or raises them. This can help invest the reader to what you have written.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>or at least a visit to the Critters like you said.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you haven't already definetly contact <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/butterfly-squad-roster">The butterfly squad</a> or go to the #critters <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/chat-guide">IRC CHAT</a>. The people available through here are great, there feedback being awesome, and of course being able to greenlight your idea. Just make sure to be <strong>polite</strong> and you'll be all good.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>That's me for now, contact me via PM if you change anything about the idea or reply to this comment, and I'd be happy to come back and take a look.</p>
<p>I would quickly recommend updating the actual idea proposal with any changes you've made, as it means critters can easily see what you've changed or are considering without having to read the comments. Otherwise have a great day/morning/afternoon/night/twilight/dusk/dawn and good luck!</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Response to reply and idea update</strong></p>
<p>You've done a good job updating the idea concept, as I can see the parts that have been changed or updated. I really hope this takes off and gets the greenlights, because I want this to exist on the main site. If you make any more changes based on feedback from others than hit me with a PM as I'd be happy to come back. I'd also love to provide draft feedback if you get the necessary greenlights.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Tiptoe&nbsp;through&nbsp;the&nbsp;window</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;by&nbsp;the&nbsp;window</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>On another note, because of your idea I went off and read SCP-4666 (Yule Man's article), and I don't know whether to say thank you or curse you. That thing was scary. I want this to succeed more now, as I want a more in-depth idea of what it might be like to experience an event. This did bring up something though. You will have to be careful with this, as to not make Yule Man seem weaker, since as of yet he hasn't been beaten. The most I think you can have them do is maybe stun him, but even doing you would have to be careful. All I'm saying is, try not weaker than he is or that he was easy to beat. What you should do is maybe make it look like a fluke, like the family got very lucky with everything that happened, enabling them to beat him. With that said, I reckon you could do a good job with this, just take the above into consideration.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Once again I wish you luck with this. Though I may ask, I know this may be repetitive and annoying, but have you contacted someone from the <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/butterfly-squad-roster">The butterfly squad</a>. If you haven't I can't stress this enough. <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/butterfly-squad-roster">The butterfly squad</a> are very good at critiquing and finding flaws, but also they can greenlight your idea, something you should know is important. Once again, I know it may be repetitive but contacting them is the best thing you can do if you haven't already. However that's me for now, and once again good luck.</p>

							</div>

