<p>Thanks for the thoughts! I've done some modifications to the &quot;Central Narrative&quot; section to take into account some of the ideas you and EthanHanson brought up.</p>
<p>Always grateful for help trying to get my thoughts in order and presentable&#8230; and to polish my vague broad stroke ideas into something usable!</p>
<p><strong>Edit based on updated thoughts</strong></p>
<p>I'm glad someone else now enjoys/suffers the Yule Man the way I do, haha. More to the point, you're also very right about needing to present the 'victory' in such a way that the Yule Man isn't just jobbing to them or 'losing' because that's what the story needs. I believe Ethan above mentioned (either here or in IRC) a thought of playing into them not so much 'winning' as 'surviving' until sun up when the Weissnacht's 'time' is over and the Yule Man is just&#8230; gone.</p>
<p>Combine that with some cleverness on their part and a few interesting parts of the Wondertainment catalog of products and I think I should be able to make the victory/survival of the event feel &quot;earned&quot; by the household (who see it to the end) without cheapening the other side of the equation.</p>
<p>I'll definitely try and keep you in the loop with this, especially when/if it gets tot he main board!</p>

